Root cause:
The bug arises from the incorrect loading order of registers in the `emit_udiv()` function when handling specific BPF instructions. When `rn` (the dividend) is loaded into `ARM_R0` (which is also used as a scratch register) before `rm` (the divisor) is loaded, it leads to both registers containing the same value, resulting in incorrect division operations.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `rm` (the divisor) is loaded into `ARM_R0` (which is also used as a scratch register) before `rn` (the dividend) is loaded. This results in both `ARM_R0` and `ARM_R1` containing the same value, leading to incorrect division operations when the `jit_udiv()` function is called.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
```
Reason:
The fix involves changing the loading order of the registers. By loading `rn` into `ARM_R1` first and then loading `rm` into `ARM_R0`, it ensures that `ARM_R0` retains the correct divisor value while `ARM_R1` holds the dividend, preventing the accidental overwriting of the dividend with the divisor.


